---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { relayOrigin } from "../../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="Local Flight is designed without advertising, behavioral analytics, cross-site tracking, or a required profile. Read what stays on your devices and what optional online features receive."
  active="privacy"
  bodyClass="document-page"
>
  <header class="document-hero terminal-frame">
    <div class="eyebrow">Beacon Tools / Privacy policy</div>
    <h1>Privacy is part of how Local Flight is built.</h1>
    <p>There is no advertising model, behavioral analytics, cross-site tracking, required Beacon profile, or sale of usage data. Settings and history normally stay on your devices, and online features connect only for a feature you choose.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top: 34px">
      <a class="action-button action-button--primary" href="/privacy/choices/">Open privacy choices <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a class="action-link" href="/support/">Contact support</a>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-footnote">Last updated September 9, 2026 · no account required · automatic diagnostics require consent</p>
  </header>

  <div class="terminal-frame document-layout">
    <nav class="document-nav" aria-label="Privacy policy sections">
      <strong>On this page</strong>
      <a href="#summary">Privacy promise and boundaries</a>
      <a href="#local">What stays on your devices</a>
      <a href="#online">What Beacon Relay receives</a>
      <a href="#remote">Encrypted Remote Companion</a>
      <a href="#modes">Mobile, Matrix, and VATSIM</a>
      <a href="#reports">Reports and diagnostics</a>
      <a href="#relay-purchases">Relay Access purchases</a>
      <a href="#support-purchases">Optional support purchases</a>
      <a href="#services">Services and legal reasons</a>
      <a href="#control">Your controls and rights</a>
    </nav>

    <article class="document-content">
      <section class="policy-section" id="summary">
        <span class="section-index">01 / THE PRIVACY PROMISE</span>
        <h2>Local first, with named online boundaries.</h2>
        <div class="summary-lines">
          <div class="summary-line"><span>01</span><strong>No profile or advertising model</strong><p>No advertising software, behavioral analytics, data brokers, cross-site tracking, or sale of usage data.</p></div>
          <div class="summary-line"><span>02</span><strong>Local by default</strong><p>Settings, saved flight boards, history, and logs normally remain on your computer or phone.</p></div>
          <div class="summary-line"><span>03</span><strong>Online by choice</strong><p>Beacon Relay, Remote Companion, Standalone, support, and diagnostics connect only for the feature you select.</p></div>
          <div class="summary-line"><span>04</span><strong>Controls you can reach</strong><p><a href="/privacy/choices/">Privacy choices</a> explains reports, resets, pairing revocation, erasure, and data requests.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="privacy-boundary-map" aria-label="Where Local Flight information lives">
          <div><span>YOUR DEVICES</span><strong>Settings · saved boards · history · logs · provider keys</strong><p>Kept on the computer or phone running Local Flight.</p></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div><span>OPTIONAL BEACON RELAY</span><strong>Service ID · requested airport or feature · access check</strong><p>Only for hosted data, encrypted remote messages, or a report you choose.</p></div>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <div><span>CHOSEN PROVIDERS</span><strong>Aviation data · payment · email · report delivery</strong><p>Used only when the related feature or transaction needs them.</p></div>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li>Companion and supported HUB75 LED matrix panels connect to the computer running Local Flight first. Remote Companion can carry encrypted messages when your home connection is unavailable; Standalone connects directly to the shared online service.</li>
          <li>Manual reports leave your device only when you send them. Automatic crash reports run only after you opt in.</li>
          <li>Optional mobile support purchases are handled by Apple or Google, unlock nothing, and do not create a Local Flight account.</li>
          <li>Local Flight is an informational display aid, not a navigation, dispatch, operational-control, or safety system.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="local">
        <span class="section-index">02 / ON YOUR DEVICES</span>
        <h2>What stays on your devices.</h2>
        <p>The computer running Local Flight keeps saved flight boards, settings, your own aviation-data API keys, movement history, logs, desktop state, paired-phone details, Remote Companion encryption secrets, and LED matrix settings. Your aviation-data keys go only to the provider you selected when Local Flight requests data from that provider. Browsers on your home or local network (LAN), Companion phones, and matrix boards ask your own Local Flight computer for their information.</p>
        <p>Real-flight Mobile Standalone keeps its relay installation ID, one device credential in SecureStore, a non-secret license summary, any pending main-device release, selected airport, appearance, diagnostics choice, pinned flight, and recent movement history on the phone. VATSIM Standalone does not create or store a Relay license or Relay credential. The real-flight credential is not duplicated in ordinary setup storage. Beacon Tools does not store that movement history.</p>
        <p>Home-screen widgets read a small saved copy already written by the mobile app. A widget does not contact your Local Flight computer, Beacon Relay, or an aviation-data provider by itself.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="online">
        <span class="section-index">03 / BEACON RELAY</span>
        <h2>What Beacon Relay receives.</h2>
        <p>If you choose Beacon Tools’ shared online service (Beacon Relay), Remote Companion, or mobile Standalone, Local Flight connects to <code>{relayOrigin}</code>. The service can verify Relay Access, provide shared airport boards and map helpers, apply fair-use limits, pass along encrypted paired-phone messages, and deliver reports you choose to send. Shared real-aircraft radar is included in Relay Access, so a radar request also sends the airport coordinates and range you are viewing.</p>
        <p>The service reuses recent shared results where possible. Normal demand can trigger at most one shared upstream schedule refresh for an airport every 15 minutes, subject to how much of the board window the provider already returned, provider availability, and spending limits. Client settings may be slower and cannot shorten that server timer. If you use your own aviation-data API keys instead, those keys stay on your Local Flight computer and are sent only to the provider you selected.</p>
        <p>Beacon Tools keeps only the service records needed to run and protect the selected feature: a random ID used to operate the service, not an account; usage counts; broad app and device details; encrypted-message routing details; last-connected times; short-lived shared data with its provider source, source-fetch time, coverage, and policy expiry; and the minimal Relay Access purchase, license, delivery, and receiver record described below. Provider-derived cache records are retained for no more than seven days by default; a shorter configured or provider limit still applies.</p>
        <p>The Local Flight application database does not store raw IP addresses. Fly.io, Cloudflare, and other network providers may briefly process connection data in infrastructure or security logs under their own policies. The service does not store your personal aviation-data keys, names or email addresses from normal app use, readable Remote Companion messages, local movement history, Standalone movement history, payment-card details, or local logs unless you explicitly include cleaned logs in a report.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="remote">
        <span class="section-index">04 / REMOTE COMPANION</span>
        <h2>Messages stay unreadable while they pass through Beacon Relay.</h2>
        <p>Remote Companion messages are end-to-end encrypted. The encryption key stays on the paired phone and Local Flight computer; Beacon Relay passes along the encrypted request and response without receiving that key or readable content.</p>
        <p>The service sees only the limited routing details needed to deliver the encrypted message. Remote Companion uses the licensed desktop host and does not create another active main-device entitlement.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="modes">
        <span class="section-index">05 / PRODUCT MODES</span>
        <h2>Mobile, LED matrices, and VATSIM.</h2>
        <h3>Companion, Standalone, and LED matrices</h3>
        <p>Companion keeps the Local Flight computer address, pairing identity, appearance, pinned flight, profiles, diagnostics choice, and optional Remote Companion access on the phone. Real-flight Standalone keeps its separate service identity, Relay Access device credential in SecureStore, non-secret license summary, airport, appearance, pinned flight, diagnostics choice, and movement history on the phone. VATSIM Standalone keeps its virtual-network settings and local display history but no Relay license or Relay credential. LED matrix boards receive only display and configuration data from your Local Flight computer.</p>
        <h3>VATSIM</h3>
        <p>VATSIM mode uses public virtual-network data for the flight board. Local Flight keeps callsigns, aircraft, filed route or flight-plan details, airport and timing data, and aircraft position where useful. It intentionally avoids storing or displaying pilot names, controller names, CIDs or account IDs, server names, and other person-identifying VATSIM fields.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="reports">
        <span class="section-index">06 / REPORTS</span>
        <h2>Reports and diagnostics remain your choice.</h2>
        <p>A manual app report can include the title and description you write, Local Flight version, public Support ID, operating system, Python version, configured airport and data mode, diagnostics choice, display settings, and the app area that sent the report.</p>
        <p>The public website contact and bug-report forms can include the fields you submit, such as an optional name and reply email, your message, product, area, version, device details, and optional text or log files. Uploaded logs have size limits, are cleaned, and are included as short excerpts rather than stored as raw attachments.</p>
        <p>For spam and duplicate protection, the service turns the connection address into a one-way code and then discards the original address. That code can accompany a support email or developer report. Support messages, report events, and duplicate-protection records may remain in the service database, support mailbox, or developer report system until routine cleanup or a valid deletion request.</p>
        <p>Automatic crash reports, when enabled, can include crash details, an error trace, the desktop or mobile area involved, and—only for the stronger diagnostics option—a short cleaned excerpt from the latest text log. Reports are cleaned on your device, checked for duplicates and rate limits by Beacon Tools, and sent to the developer’s Linear report inbox.</p>
        <p>Diagnostics do not intentionally include API keys, activation tokens, raw install IDs, raw IP addresses, screenshots, recordings, stored flight history, full local logs, or account data.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="relay-purchases">
        <span class="section-index">07 / RELAY ACCESS PURCHASES</span>
        <h2>Paying for hosted access does not turn Local Flight into an account.</h2>
        <p>Local Flight desktop and mobile apps remain free. Every new supported Stripe, App Store, or Google Play annual subscription creates one separate, portable Beacon Relay Access entitlement for one active main device. Cancellation retains access through the provider-confirmed paid period, and authoritative grace is honored only through its reported boundary. Existing verified lifetime purchases, complimentary grants, and eligible founder installs retain permanent access. An entitlement can move between desktop Beacon Relay and mobile Standalone with confirmation. The old main device remains active until the new credential is safely stored and committed. LAN Companion uses no Relay entitlement; Remote Companion uses its licensed desktop host.</p>
        <p>Stripe, Apple, or Google processes the payment. A checkout or recovery email is used for delivery, protection, recovery, or a device move; it is not a conventional Beacon account or public profile. Beacon Tools does not receive payment-card details, Apple ID, or Google account identity.</p>
        <details class="technical-details">
          <summary>Technical purchase, delivery, and recovery details</summary>
          <p>For Stripe checkout, Beacon Tools receives the checkout email so it can deliver a license key and recovery link. The relay stores a keyed one-way email lookup plus encrypted material needed for recovery, notifications, and authoritative provider reconciliation. Purchase identities, master keys, receiver credentials, and events are stored as keyed hashes or masked references. Plain email, raw keys, device credentials, plaintext purchase tokens, payment-card details, and store-account identities are not written to the Relay Access database or application logs.</p>
          <p>The notification outbox keeps masked references and delivery state, with temporary destinations and delivery material encrypted using a separate versioned key. Provider acknowledgements and reconciliation are also queued durably. Expired access challenges and old successful notification or provider-event records are periodically removed while the minimal purchase and activation audit remains available for service, fraud, refund, and recovery needs.</p>
          <p>On iOS, an explicit setup or restore action obtains Apple’s signed StoreKit subscription evidence. Beacon Relay verifies its signature, expected app and product identity, environment, ownership, period, grace, renewal, and revocation state, then reconciles it through Apple’s server API and notifications. On Android, real-flight Standalone verifies the annual subscription through the Google Play Developer API. Beacon Relay checks package, product, purchase and acknowledgement state, period boundaries, and subscription lineage, then uses authenticated real-time developer notifications for later changes. Earlier paid-app and non-consumable ownership can be checked only for founder/legacy restoration. Play Integrity is used only to confirm the official Android app when a handoff moves an existing portable license. Raw signed proofs are processed transiently; provider reconciliation handles are retained only encrypted.</p>
          <p>Email is optional for mobile ownership until you choose recovery, protection, or transfer. Protection first sends an address-confirmation link without the key. After confirmation, the management page can reveal the existing key once and Beacon Tools queues the same key for email; this initial delivery does not rotate it. The one-time secret is carried in the URL fragment so it is not sent to the hosting service as part of the page request. Short-lived private email links provide access to the individual licenses associated with that address; there is no Beacon Tools password, profile, or conventional account. Refunds, disputes, purchase revocation, abuse controls, or a required provider-policy shutdown can disable hosted access. Payment never overrides an aviation-data provider agreement.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="support-purchases">
        <span class="section-index">08 / OPTIONAL SUPPORT PURCHASES</span>
        <h2>Support purchases are tips, not Relay Access.</h2>
        <p>The mobile app can offer three optional one-time consumable support products through Apple App Store or Google Play. They unlock nothing and create no lasting paid entitlement. They create no subscription, paywall, or Local Flight account. The store shows its localized price before its purchase sheet opens.</p>
        <p>Apple or Google processes the purchase, payment-card details, store account, refunds, and store purchase history under its own terms. Local Flight does not receive your card details, Apple ID, or Google account identity.</p>
        <details class="technical-details">
          <summary>Technical support-purchase verification and retention details</summary>
          <p>After the store reports a successful support purchase, Local Flight sends the store platform, product ID, random Local Flight installation identifier, app version, bundle or package identifier, and either the Apple transaction ID or Google purchase token over HTTPS to Beacon Relay. The service checks that support purchase with Apple’s App Store Server API or the Google Play Developer API.</p>
          <p>The app finishes or consumes the purchase only after verification succeeds. If verification is interrupted, the unfinished purchase remains with the store so Local Flight can try again. A pending, cancelled, rejected, refunded, or revoked purchase does not unlock or change any Local Flight feature.</p>
          <p>Beacon Relay immediately turns the support-purchase proof into a keyed one-way hash and short reference. Its verification record keeps only that hash and reference, product ID, store platform and environment, installation fingerprint, verification status, first and last attempt times, attempt count, and a short error code when verification fails. A one-way network code may also be used for rate limits and abuse protection; the original connection address is discarded from the Local Flight application database.</p>
          <p>Beacon Tools does not retain the raw Apple transaction ID, signed Apple transaction body, raw Google purchase token, price, currency, payment-card details, or store-account identity. Purchase evidence is never added to diagnostics, reports, heartbeats, or operator dashboards.</p>
        </details>
        <h3>Your choices and requests</h3>
        <p>You can use every Local Flight feature without making a support purchase; these tips remain unrelated to Relay Access. Local Flight desktop also remains usable without Relay Access through Bring Your Own Keys or VATSIM. Removing the app does not erase records held by Apple or Google; use the store’s own controls for store purchase history or refunds. For a Beacon Tools data request, use the Support ID shown by Local Flight and, if available, the short purchase reference or approximate purchase time. See <a href="/privacy/choices/">Privacy choices</a> or contact <a href="mailto:privacy@beacontools.cc">privacy@beacontools.cc</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="services">
        <span class="section-index">09 / SERVICES AND LEGAL REASONS</span>
        <h2>Other services are used only when your setup needs them.</h2>
        <p>Depending on your choices, Local Flight may communicate with AeroDataBox, AviationStack, ADS-B Exchange or RapidAPI, OpenSky, VATSIM, aviationweather.gov, OurAirports, OpenStreetMap or Overpass, public terrain tile sources, Linear for consent-based report triage, the configured mailbox provider for website contact messages, and Apple App Store or Google Play for app ownership or optional purchases.</p>
        <p>Requests made with your own aviation-data keys use those locally stored keys. Beacon Relay paths use the hosted relay and shared cache. Provider data and API access remain governed separately from Local Flight's MIT software license; see the <a href="https://github.com/BeaconTools/local-flight/blob/main/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md">third-party notices</a>. Stripe processes website Relay Access purchases. See <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy">Stripe</a>, <a href="https://linear.app/privacy">Linear</a>, <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">Apple</a>, and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google</a> privacy information.</p>
        <p>Where data-protection law applies, Beacon Tools relies on legitimate interests for the limited service-operation and security records described here, consent for automatic diagnostics you enable, and your request when processing support messages or manual reports. Optional mobile purchases use the minimum transaction details needed to perform the purchase you requested, prevent duplicate processing, and meet store and security requirements. Apple or Google processes the payment separately under its own store terms.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-section" id="control">
        <span class="section-index">10 / YOUR CONTROLS</span>
        <h2>Your controls and rights.</h2>
        <h3>Email and support</h3>
        <p>Local Flight does not require an email address during normal app use. An email is used when you buy Relay Access through Stripe or voluntarily protect, recover, or transfer a license. Beacon Relay stores its keyed lookup and encrypted notification copy rather than a plain email field. If you use a Beacon Tools website form or email Beacon Tools directly, the optional reply email and message you provide are handled by the relay and mailbox provider so Beacon Tools can reply.</p>
        <h3>Reset and erasure</h3>
        <p>Your local app data is under your control. Reset Setup is different from erasing all desktop, Linux, or Raspberry Pi data. Mobile reset, Remote Companion revocation, and full app removal are also separate actions. See <a href="/privacy/choices/">privacy choices</a> for the exact steps.</p>
        <h3>Data rights</h3>
        <p>You may have rights to access, correct, erase, restrict, object to, or obtain a portable copy of personal data. Local Flight has no accounts, so include the Support ID shown by the app, a report reference, an approximate time, or the reply email you supplied when asking Beacon Tools to locate a record. Contact <a href="mailto:privacy@beacontools.cc">privacy@beacontools.cc</a> or use <a href="/privacy/choices/">Privacy choices</a>. You may also contact your local data-protection authority.</p>
      </section>
    </article>

    <aside class="document-aside">
      <strong>Want to change something?</strong>
      <p>Diagnostics, pairing revocation, reset, full erasure, and privacy request steps are collected on one page.</p>
      <a class="action-link" href="/privacy/choices/">Privacy choices</a>
    </aside>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
